### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### evaluate - CS203-20   (saves an impact record per activity)
### checks every activity in an itinerary against one news article
### one model call per activity, so allow a few seconds per activity
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

### japan ski trip vs the hokkaido earthquake, expect impacted true
POST http://localhost:8080/api/impact/evaluate
Content-Type: application/json

{
    "itineraryId": 1,
    "newsId": 1
}

### singapore weekend vs the same earthquake, expect impacted false for everything
### this is the one that proves the model is reading the article
POST http://localhost:8080/api/impact/evaluate
Content-Type: application/json

{
    "itineraryId": 3,
    "newsId": 1
}

### bangkok holiday vs the bangkok flooding, expect impacted true
POST http://localhost:8080/api/impact/evaluate
Content-Type: application/json

{
    "itineraryId": 2,
    "newsId": 2
}

### unknown itinerary, expect 404
POST http://localhost:8080/api/impact/evaluate
Content-Type: application/json

{
    "itineraryId": 999,
    "newsId": 1
}


### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### update profile and change password   (uses the seeded user dave)
### both need a login AND the CSRF header (X-XSRF-TOKEN), because they are not GETs
###
### how the token works: the server sets a cookie called XSRF-TOKEN, and every POST, PUT
### and PATCH must send the same value in the X-XSRF-TOKEN header. The client keeps the
### cookie by itself. You copy its value into @xsrf below by hand.
###
### steps, once per session:
###   1. send "csrf probe" (a 401 is expected). In the response headers find
###        Set-Cookie: XSRF-TOKEN=<value>; Path=/
###      and paste only <value> (stop before the first ;) into @xsrf
###   2. send "login as dave"
###   3. send "who am I" (expect 200). If its response sets a NEW XSRF-TOKEN cookie, paste the
###      new value into @xsrf. Spring can change the token at login
###   4. send the requests you want
###
### seeded users all use password123 (admin uses admin123). If you get 401 on login, the
### database may hold old or damaged password hashes: see fix-seed-passwords.sql
###
### these requests change the real database (data.sql is INSERT OR IGNORE, so it will not
### reset them). Change things back afterwards or restore data/tripsense.db
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

### the host is 127.0.0.1 instead of localhost on purpose. REST Client keeps cookies between runs,
### and once it already holds an XSRF-TOKEN cookie the server stops sending it, so you could never
### see the value. Cookies are stored per host name, so 127.0.0.1 gives these requests a clean jar.
@host = http://127.0.0.1:8080
@xsrf = paste-the-xsrf-token-cookie-value-here

### csrf probe, expect 401 with a Set-Cookie: XSRF-TOKEN=... header
GET {{host}}/api/auth/me

### login as dave (password123), expect 200 {"role":"USER"}
POST {{host}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{xsrf}}

email=dave@example.com&password=password123

### who am I, expect username dave (run again after a rename to see the new name)
GET {{host}}/api/auth/me

### rename, expect 200 and the new username in the body
PATCH {{host}}/api/user/me
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{xsrf}}

{
    "username": "davey"
}

### rename to a username another account has, expect 409
PATCH {{host}}/api/user/me
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{xsrf}}

{
    "username": "alice"
}

### invalid username, expect 400
PATCH {{host}}/api/user/me
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{xsrf}}

{
    "username": "a!"
}

### wrong current password, expect 400
PUT {{host}}/api/user/me/password
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{xsrf}}

{
    "currentPassword": "not-my-password",
    "newPassword": "newpassword123"
}

### change password, expect 204
### every session of this account is ended, so the next request returns 401 until you log in again
PUT {{host}}/api/user/me/password
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{xsrf}}

{
    "currentPassword": "password123",
    "newPassword": "newpassword123"
}

### after the change: who am I, expect 401 because the session was ended
GET {{host}}/api/auth/me

### after the change: the old password no longer logs in, expect 401
POST {{host}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{xsrf}}

email=dave@example.com&password=password123

### after the change: the new password logs in, expect 200
POST {{host}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{xsrf}}

email=dave@example.com&password=newpassword123

### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### recommendations - CS203-23
### needs a login first, see the login block above for how to fill in @xsrf
### generate is a POST so it needs the header, reading them back is a plain GET
###
### generate only calls ollama for impacts it has not seen before. an impact that
### already has a recommendation is served straight from the db, so a second run is
### instant and gives the same answer
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

### karwei's hokkaido trip, expect the 4 seeded recommendations worst first
GET {{host}}/api/itinerary/1/recommendation

### the bangkok trip, this is the one where distance ranking shows its work
GET {{host}}/api/itinerary/2/recommendation

### generate for the bangkok trip, expect 200 and a list
### with ollama stopped this still answers, it falls back to the top of our own shortlist
POST {{host}}/api/recommendation/generate
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{xsrf}}

{
    "itineraryId": 2
}

### validation, expect 400 because itineraryId is required
POST {{host}}/api/recommendation/generate
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{xsrf}}

{
}

### a trip that does not exist, expect 404 and a readable message
POST {{host}}/api/recommendation/generate
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{xsrf}}

{
    "itineraryId": 999
}
